<h2>Wind Conditions in Temple and Bell County</h2>
<p>Most damaging wind in the Temple area comes from severe thunderstorms rather than hurricanes. The National Weather Service considers thunderstorm gusts of 58 mph or higher severe, and spring storms in Central Texas regularly reach that range, sometimes with downbursts that flatten fences and snap trees along a narrow path. Fall storms and strong cold fronts can bring damaging gusts as well.</p>
<p>Local conditions matter. Homes on open lots in newer subdivisions on the edges of Temple, and lakeside properties near Lake Belton with open exposure, tend to catch more direct wind. Older central Temple neighborhoods with mature trees face a different risk: falling limbs. Summer heat adds another factor, since it both softens sealant strips and, over years, makes shingles brittle and more prone to creasing. Check our <a href="/weather/">local weather page</a> to see recent wind events in your area.</p>

<h2>When to Call for a Wind Damage Inspection</h2>
<p>Call after any storm with strong gusts, especially if you find shingle pieces on the ground, notice lifted edges, or see debris on the roof. Even a few missing shingles leave underlayment exposed to sun and rain, and broken seals can let wind peel away larger sections in the next storm. If hail or heavy rain came with the wind, our <a href="/services/storm-damage-roof-repair-temple-tx/">storm damage roof repair</a> service covers the full post-storm assessment, and the <a href="/tools/storm-damage-checklist/">storm damage checklist</a> helps you organize photos and notes beforehand.</p>
